WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.100
Hi, this is Steve Kaufmann again.

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:06.840
Uh, and today I have a special guest, very
special because she's not the usual sort

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.970
of just language learning type of person.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:15.240
Jo Franco has a channel on YouTube
where she talks about many things,

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.270
including language learning,
but also things of the heart.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.510
And so I'm going to talk to
her about attitude and language

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.010
learning, and she's also a polyglot.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.690
So we'll get into all of this, but first.

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.395
Uh, if you enjoy my
channel, please subscribe.

00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:32.655
If you listen to me in a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:33.525 --> 00:00:36.075
Hello, Jo, how are you?

00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:37.695
I'm very well.

00:00:37.695 --> 00:00:38.175
Thank you.

00:00:38.355 --> 00:00:39.915
First of all, where are you located?

00:00:40.365 --> 00:00:41.235
Geographically?

00:00:41.415 --> 00:00:46.575
Currently geographically based in
London, strategic, partially for

00:00:46.575 --> 00:00:48.585
language learning the other travel.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.790
Which to me goes hand in hand and language
has always been the biggest gateway

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:59.400
to experiencing the most authentic
life enriching moments of my life.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.640
And so I figured, Hey, London
is pretty much the most

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.290
international city in the world.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:08.010
So every day here I'm like
ordering a coffee in Italian.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:12.660
I'm ordering lunch in French, I'm
speaking Portuguese at the gym,

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.180
and that's only in a day's time.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.010
So it's really exciting.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.950
Uh, you say, and of course I,
obviously, I agree with you that...

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.250
I don't need these now I've
got everything working...

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:29.700
um, that the language is a gateway
to so much, you know, and, uh,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.160
you know, some people think, oh,
I gotta learn it, you know, why

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.330
would you learn some other language?

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.229
All you need is English.

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:38.580
And so some people have a very sort
of utilitarian approach to language.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.590
Like I only learn something if I need it.

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:45.870
In fact, until you get into it, you
don't realize just how rich a world is

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.850
available to you if you learn languages.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:53.070
So tell us which languages do you speak
and what have been some of your, uh,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.410
pleasant experiences in these languages?

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.490
Great question.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:59.430
I mean, we could talk about
this for the rest of time.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:04.045
It's an endless pool, like you
really can just keep going for life.

00:02:04.435 --> 00:02:07.105
And obviously you've shown that
through your work, but in my

00:02:07.105 --> 00:02:11.305
experience, so I actually never
was supposed to speak English.

00:02:11.335 --> 00:02:15.835
I was born in Brazil ... a
hundred percent Brazilian.

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:15.985
Right.

00:02:16.525 --> 00:02:18.205
And no one in my family was American.

00:02:18.205 --> 00:02:20.665
So the fact that we immigrated
when I was five years old was

00:02:20.665 --> 00:02:21.975
kind of a shock to everyone.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.160
So, um, Brazil speaking Portuguese.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.200
I moved to a town in Connecticut.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.230
I had to learn English, no one
spoke Portuguese around me.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:31.880
They barely even spoke Spanish.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:36.590
So that was actually a really big
moment in my language learning career.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.430
And I feel like I've tried to
replicate as much of that survival

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:41.690
mentality to my language learning.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:46.650
Because I learned English at record speeds
cause I had to, I had no other choice.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.470
Uh, and so I spoke,
obviously I speak English.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:55.380
That's my main language day to day and
how I kind of think, then I started

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.440
learning French in middle school, which...

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.480
it's middle school French, you
know, you're not going to be fluent

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.790
from that, but I really loved it.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:03.990
I just loved it.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:08.190
I fell in love with the little pictures
of the cafes in the textbooks and I'm

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:12.480
like, man, maybe one day I can be a
person sitting at one of those cafes.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:13.950
I don't know what it was.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.940
There was just something about
the European cafe lifestyle

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.180
that I was obsessed with before
I even drink coffee, you know?

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.130
And then in high school I was very
privileged to be in a high school

00:03:26.145 --> 00:03:27.585
where they taught Italian as well.

00:03:28.005 --> 00:03:31.545
So I had to write a letter to the
principal and say, Hey, I grew

00:03:31.545 --> 00:03:34.965
up bilingual can you please let
me take two languages in school?

00:03:34.965 --> 00:03:36.645
Which was something that was not allowed.

00:03:36.735 --> 00:03:38.325
They didn't want the
kids getting confused.

00:03:38.775 --> 00:03:42.555
So I broke the rules, very
inline with me as a human being.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:45.825
And I started learning Italian
and all of these are kind of grade

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:50.685
school, elementary school classes, not
learning too much, but then I graduated

00:03:50.685 --> 00:03:52.035
and I had never traveled before.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.060
I could not travel.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.770
We were undocumented for 12 years.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.210
So all of this time, it's
a pretty crazy story.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.150
I was in preparation...

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.390
I was like in the books doing
my little grammar tables.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.360
Cause one day I would live a
global life that was in my mind.

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.240
I didn't even have a green
card, let alone a passport.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:13.740
So this was all in my mind.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:19.230
But right before college, I was able
to get my green card applied to a

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:23.855
college and went to this school in
downtown Manhattan, where I knew

00:04:23.855 --> 00:04:25.355
it would be a global experience.

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:26.945
Can I stop you for a sec?

00:04:27.545 --> 00:04:30.035
Because you said a few things
that I want to comment on before

00:04:30.035 --> 00:04:34.175
I forget, you know, at my advanced
age, I forget things very easily.

00:04:34.655 --> 00:04:37.565
Uh, you said that you arrived
in the US at the age of five.

00:04:38.525 --> 00:04:40.475
I arrived in Canada at the age of five.

00:04:41.025 --> 00:04:44.670
I have no recollection of
transitioning from Swedish to English.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.330
I was just with, I don't know what
language I spoke when I, you know,

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:53.310
first, you know, played with kids, but
in no time I had forgotten my Swedish

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.990
and I was just, I just spoke English.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.080
So at the age of five, we
transition very, very quickly.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.240
Uh, that was one thing I wanted to say.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:01.200
What was the other thing about?

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:01.950
Mm.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:02.430
Oh, yes.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.400
It's interesting that at school
they tell you how many languages

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:06.860
and which languages you can learn.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:07.979
That's so silly.

00:05:08.159 --> 00:05:11.880
They should allow kids to explore
whatever language they want to explore.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.909
So there was something else that I
wanted to comment on, but I've forgotten.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.429
So here we are, you're in college.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.229
So take us to the next stage.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.250
We're going to talk about this on my
podcast, too, this whole transitioning

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.020
and the brain and growing up bilingual.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.909
So got to college and I didn't.

00:05:27.909 --> 00:05:30.855
take any French classes in college at all.

00:05:30.915 --> 00:05:33.075
Maybe I did half of a
semester, but I didn't love it.

00:05:33.465 --> 00:05:35.865
Didn't like the teacher
didn't like the lessons.

00:05:35.865 --> 00:05:39.495
I didn't, I just didn't, I'm not
very much of a rule follower, right?

00:05:39.495 --> 00:05:42.555
So the idea that I'm sitting in
this classroom where people were

00:05:42.555 --> 00:05:46.635
just trying to fulfill some language
credits, it was BS in my opinion.

00:05:47.475 --> 00:05:50.985
So I didn't, I didn't really love
that, but I still decided to study

00:05:50.985 --> 00:05:52.825
abroad in Paris my sophomore year.

00:05:53.315 --> 00:05:55.815
Which is when my French
really went to the next level.

00:05:57.825 --> 00:06:01.244
I was living in a homestay with
this mom who spoke no English.

00:06:01.244 --> 00:06:02.114
And that's what I wanted.

00:06:02.205 --> 00:06:06.284
I'm like, I want a struggle, you know,
like taking me back to that survival mode.

00:06:06.914 --> 00:06:11.054
And at the same time, I started this
crazy YouTube channel with my friends from

00:06:11.054 --> 00:06:15.344
college, where we were just documenting
our French experiences, traveling,

00:06:15.405 --> 00:06:17.234
traveling around, taking cheap flights.

00:06:17.864 --> 00:06:21.849
To me, it was all so foreign
for lack of a better word.

00:06:21.849 --> 00:06:23.409
Like I didn't grow up traveling.

00:06:23.409 --> 00:06:25.570
So I wanted to film everything.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:29.170
People ask like, oh, why
are you so enthusiastic?

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.000
You know, that's the, one of
the first things you said to me.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:36.420
It's because I guess everything that
happened in my life is so crazy that I'm

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:40.380
kind of like, oh yeah, I have to film
this because I might never be able to

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:42.200
do it again, but it became my career.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:45.030
Can I stop you here before I
forget two points that I want

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:46.080
to make and then we'll continue?

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.570
So now I remember what I wanted to say.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:51.810
You said that, uh, you had these
textbooks, French textbooks

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:53.130
with pictures of cafes.

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:56.880
I got turned onto French at McGill
university because we had these

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.930
textbooks and I got turned onto these
pictures of, you know, 17th and 18th

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:03.360
century French people and whatever.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:04.770
So it doesn't matter what it is.

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:10.599
Uh, you contrast the role of a teacher
or a textbook that can actually

00:07:10.599 --> 00:07:11.979
turn someone on to the language.

00:07:11.979 --> 00:07:16.570
You contrast that with writing exams
every year, uh, which is what you

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:17.950
experienced, what I experienced.

00:07:17.979 --> 00:07:20.920
And you know, if you're a good student,
you're going to pass these exams, you're

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.919
going to get eight out of 10 or whatever
it might be, and you still can't speak.

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:28.180
So you've got on the one hand, the role
of the teacher is not to teach something

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.875
and test the student on it and the
better students get better marks and

00:07:31.875 --> 00:07:33.645
the weaker students gets poor marks.

00:07:33.795 --> 00:07:34.695
It doesn't matter.

00:07:34.995 --> 00:07:39.795
The role is to sort of trigger
this enthusiasm, which I think we

00:07:39.795 --> 00:07:43.335
both have enthusiasm for discovery
for languages and so forth.

00:07:43.335 --> 00:07:46.635
So I wanted to make those
two comments back to you now.

00:07:46.695 --> 00:07:48.795
So you're traveling and
you're enthusiastic.

00:07:49.155 --> 00:07:52.844
An amazing point to make, because when
you think about language learning, that

00:07:52.844 --> 00:07:54.914
is our, those are hours of your life.

00:07:54.945 --> 00:07:57.735
That's so much dedication, you
could be doing so many other things.

00:07:58.094 --> 00:08:01.155
So if you're going to dedicate time,
you need to be excited about it.

00:08:01.515 --> 00:08:05.245
There's a reason why people are kind of
downloading these apps and they're like,

00:08:05.265 --> 00:08:06.405
oh yeah, I kind of want to learn it.

00:08:06.615 --> 00:08:08.445
But what's the reason why?

00:08:08.445 --> 00:08:11.645
Like what's, what are you
obsessed about with that langauge?

00:08:11.835 --> 00:08:15.405
Or with that idea of your
life transforming because you

00:08:15.405 --> 00:08:18.955
learn that language and that
to me was always the "why".

00:08:19.215 --> 00:08:23.224
I was like, if I learn this language,
I can live a different life.

00:08:23.405 --> 00:08:28.565
And that's so powerful in my opinion,
that it would get me excited about doing

00:08:28.565 --> 00:08:32.885
grammar charts or whatever, but like doing
the grammar for grammar's sake is not

00:08:32.944 --> 00:08:34.485
what I'm going to be spending my time on.

00:08:34.985 --> 00:08:37.825
So I love that yeah, you pointed that out.

00:08:37.825 --> 00:08:38.215
It's true.

00:08:38.215 --> 00:08:41.905
It's gotta be something that you're
passionate about and a teacher, they can

00:08:41.905 --> 00:08:46.195
point you in the right directions, but
really that's an individual experience.

00:08:46.795 --> 00:08:52.525
So for all of my languages, for the most
part, it was the culture excited me.

00:08:52.825 --> 00:08:56.365
It was the access that I would
get because I would speak this

00:08:56.365 --> 00:08:57.385
language and unlock things.

00:08:57.805 --> 00:09:00.745
So that's kind of how I learned
French perfected, not perfected,

00:09:00.745 --> 00:09:04.885
but like really got fluent enough
where I can do life and French.

00:09:05.515 --> 00:09:07.075
And then Italian, same thing.

00:09:07.075 --> 00:09:10.645
I actually loved the fact that Italian
was only spoken in Italy for the most

00:09:10.645 --> 00:09:13.525
part, because it was not really useful.

00:09:13.915 --> 00:09:16.525
And that was exciting to me because
I'm like, oh, I'm only learning this

00:09:16.525 --> 00:09:22.520
language because I love Italy and Italians
and that feels sacred and special.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.300
Whereas the other languages
like Spanish, they always say,

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:27.350
oh, you got to learn Spanish.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:29.960
I actually avoided learning
Spanish for the longest time.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:33.650
Funny enough, I'm like, I don't want
to learn it because I speak Portuguese,

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:37.760
I can already kind of understand it,
but joke was on me because when I got

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:42.890
back from my study abroad semester, the
first project that we were able to go

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:47.630
on and work to make promotional YouTube
videos for was a Spanish language school.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:51.925
So for three months I, I
had to go to Spanish school.

00:09:52.225 --> 00:09:53.875
And in that summer I learned Spanish.

00:09:54.235 --> 00:09:56.395
So that's when Spanish
kind of added into the mix.

00:09:56.485 --> 00:09:57.835
Stop you there because I have a question.

00:09:58.315 --> 00:10:02.215
I have found it very difficult and my
Spanish is much better than my Portuguese

00:10:02.755 --> 00:10:04.915
and the languages are so similar.

00:10:04.945 --> 00:10:09.325
They're like 90% of the same
words, 90%, the same grammar, of

00:10:09.325 --> 00:10:10.585
course, different pronunciation.

00:10:10.915 --> 00:10:11.485
And it's so...

00:10:11.515 --> 00:10:12.595
I find it so difficult.

00:10:12.595 --> 00:10:16.365
Like if I learn a totally different
language, there's no interference,

00:10:16.845 --> 00:10:22.065
but it's so difficult to get out
of this ... thing into that other

00:10:22.065 --> 00:10:25.725
language, which, and you got to shut
out the one that you speak better.

00:10:25.935 --> 00:10:29.565
So coming at it from Portuguese,
was that an obstacle for you?

00:10:29.565 --> 00:10:33.435
The fact that basically you understand
that you even know you can communicate

00:10:33.435 --> 00:10:36.525
in Portuguese and they'll understand
you, does that prevent you from

00:10:36.525 --> 00:10:38.085
throwing yourself into Spanish?

00:10:38.745 --> 00:10:40.439
It's such an amazing question.

00:10:40.439 --> 00:10:43.590
It was traumatic the first week in Mexico.

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:47.730
I was, I was like wanting to
bite my tongue every five seconds

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.200
because I'm like, what is it?

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:50.580
What is it?

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:54.330
Is it ... is it ...? Like I was just
losing my mind because it wasn't

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:57.660
just Portuguese that was interfering,
but it was Italian as well.

00:10:59.345 --> 00:11:02.915
Anytime I speak Spanish
I'm basically speaking...

00:11:02.945 --> 00:11:05.405
now I'm basically speaking Italian.

00:11:05.405 --> 00:11:08.135
Like it's Spanish with Italian.

00:11:08.525 --> 00:11:12.395
In the beginning it was definitely
Portuguese seeping into my Spanish

00:11:12.815 --> 00:11:14.405
and then I did have to block it out.

00:11:14.435 --> 00:11:19.595
You have to kind of like find a way
to isolate the language in your brain.

00:11:20.195 --> 00:11:25.890
Uh, which is funny because now after many
years of, of speaking those languages

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:29.220
and I started making videos in those
languages, which really, really helped.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:33.180
So on the old YouTube channel, which
we had over a million subscribers,

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:38.230
we would post three videos a week,
Tuesdays in English, Fridays in

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.050
French Sundays in Portuguese.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:45.030
And then I would travel alone and go
to Italy and make videos in Italian.

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:49.920
And so this kind of went on where I was
not only filming myself for a whole day,

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.590
speaking a language, but editing myself.

00:11:52.615 --> 00:11:54.835
And having to look up
like, is that even right?

00:11:54.835 --> 00:11:55.795
Is that how you say that?

00:11:56.275 --> 00:12:00.805
And it's just, it's a secret
weapon, Steve, where I'm like, okay,

00:12:00.865 --> 00:12:03.865
there's some magic that happens
when you start filming yourself.

00:12:04.225 --> 00:12:08.125
And it's convenient that this is
what I ended up doing for a living.

00:12:08.635 --> 00:12:10.075
So this brings us to now.

00:12:10.405 --> 00:12:14.234
I stopped actively learning
languages for a long time.

00:12:14.295 --> 00:12:14.655
Right?

00:12:14.655 --> 00:12:18.944
Like I think all of those languages
were in my mind by the age of 24.

00:12:19.305 --> 00:12:20.775
I didn't have to do much work.

00:12:21.104 --> 00:12:22.425
You know how it is, you go through...

00:12:22.665 --> 00:12:24.055
You're already 24?

00:12:24.525 --> 00:12:25.635
I'm 29.

00:12:26.205 --> 00:12:27.194
I'm 29.

00:12:28.814 --> 00:12:37.455
As I was 24 these languages were in my
mind and I didn't really make the time to

00:12:37.455 --> 00:12:42.075
learn new languages because I was getting
around most of the world with no issues.

00:12:42.075 --> 00:12:45.135
Like I would be in Hungary and of
course I didn't speak Hungarian,

00:12:45.135 --> 00:12:47.985
but the woman, I would be, I'd
be like, do you speak English?

00:12:47.985 --> 00:12:48.255
No.

00:12:48.405 --> 00:12:49.064
Do you speak French?

00:12:49.095 --> 00:12:49.305
No.

00:12:49.425 --> 00:12:50.175
Do you speak Portuguese?

00:12:50.175 --> 00:12:50.295
No.

00:12:50.305 --> 00:12:50.955
Do you speak Spanish?

00:12:50.985 --> 00:12:51.194
No.

00:12:51.465 --> 00:12:54.285
You speak Italian?

00:12:54.725 --> 00:12:56.885
... and it was amazing.

00:12:56.885 --> 00:13:01.444
So I'm like, okay, I don't necessarily
spend much time learning new languages

00:13:01.865 --> 00:13:04.865
and maintaining the ones that I know,
then something really interesting

00:13:04.865 --> 00:13:09.905
happened last year during the
pandemic where it was the first time

00:13:09.905 --> 00:13:11.765
in a long time, I wasn't traveling.

00:13:12.214 --> 00:13:16.115
And I was kind of bummed in
quarantine, like the rest of the world.

00:13:16.625 --> 00:13:19.845
And I realized, I am so sad.

00:13:19.995 --> 00:13:22.305
The only thing that's gonna
make me excited is going to

00:13:22.305 --> 00:13:23.625
be learning a new language.

00:13:24.165 --> 00:13:26.685
And so I had flirted around
with Egyptian Arabic.

00:13:26.775 --> 00:13:30.405
My best friend is Egyptin, and I
just decided I'm going to book 20

00:13:30.405 --> 00:13:34.125
hours of classes online with this
complete stranger who turned into

00:13:34.125 --> 00:13:36.125
a dear friend who lives in Egypt.

00:13:36.765 --> 00:13:41.005
And every day of last year, like for
the first two months, I would meet

00:13:41.005 --> 00:13:43.285
up with him online 45 minute lessons.

00:13:43.824 --> 00:13:46.645
And I started learning Egyptian
Arabic to the point where I'm like

00:13:49.885 --> 00:13:53.635
...
like, and I started communicating
with my best friend and her family

00:13:53.935 --> 00:13:55.495
who I had known for 10 years.

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:58.880
And that opened up this
new Egyptian Arabic door.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:03.440
Uh, and at the same time
I had dabbled in Greek.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:06.470
I'm like, oh yeah, ... blah, blah, blah.

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:11.630
But then I'm like, I know that if I
dedicate my time to this language,

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:15.475
to both of these languages, I will
be able to be more conversational.

00:14:16.165 --> 00:14:19.315
And so I just started posting more
content about it on my Instagram.

00:14:19.675 --> 00:14:21.505
So I used to be primarily on YouTube.

00:14:21.535 --> 00:14:22.225
Now I've shifted.

00:14:22.225 --> 00:14:23.275
I'm definitely on both.

00:14:23.935 --> 00:14:26.335
And I started this 21
day language challenge.

00:14:26.365 --> 00:14:27.895
We're on day 17 today.

00:14:28.645 --> 00:14:33.445
And on Instagram, every single day, I
post a video of me reading an entry in

00:14:33.445 --> 00:14:38.575
Greek and posing my audience the same
question in whatever their language, you

00:14:38.575 --> 00:14:42.870
know, their target language is and that's
improved my Greek tenfold, you know?

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:47.069
Like in these short three weeks, I'm
like, shoot, this is what happens

00:14:47.069 --> 00:14:50.699
when you dedicate some time and
energy into these languages, you

00:14:50.699 --> 00:14:55.860
know, you open you open these doors,
so that's my linguistic journey.

00:14:56.569 --> 00:14:57.240
Okay.

00:14:57.329 --> 00:15:00.750
So, because we're going to have another
conversation on your channel I'd like

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:05.579
to sort of summarize and maybe make some
comments for the purpose of my listeners

00:15:05.579 --> 00:15:07.470
and what I get from our conversation.

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:10.680
Uh, I share some of the problems you have.

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:12.740
In other words, I would like
to learn a hundred languages.

00:15:13.255 --> 00:15:14.954
There's only so much time in the day.

00:15:15.344 --> 00:15:20.084
And, uh, we're always conscious of, uh,
some of the languages we have learned

00:15:20.115 --> 00:15:23.444
are not as good as they once were,
or we'd like to be better at them and

00:15:23.444 --> 00:15:24.795
we just don't have the time to do it.

00:15:25.155 --> 00:15:27.584
So, you know, it's like everything
about language learning, there

00:15:27.584 --> 00:15:32.175
is no perfection, you can't learn
every language to a super level.

00:15:32.604 --> 00:15:34.515
It's fun to explore new languages.

00:15:34.785 --> 00:15:36.314
And so that's a good problem to have.

00:15:36.675 --> 00:15:38.944
So that's the one thing we're
quite similar in that regard.

00:15:39.584 --> 00:15:41.834
Uh, the second thing is your enthusiasm.

00:15:41.834 --> 00:15:45.165
And I think, you know, to be successful
in anything, not just language learning,

00:15:45.165 --> 00:15:47.265
you have to be passionate about it.

00:15:47.265 --> 00:15:48.944
You have to be enthusiastic about it.

00:15:49.334 --> 00:15:53.805
That enthusiasm can partly come
from within, but also I think

00:15:53.805 --> 00:15:58.605
it's a matter of being open to
new stimulus, new opportunities.

00:15:58.935 --> 00:16:04.155
Uh, and so the, sometimes the, the
trigger can come from somewhere else.

00:16:04.185 --> 00:16:07.665
Like it could be a textbook as in
your case, but, uh, I think it's a

00:16:07.665 --> 00:16:09.435
wonderful, you're a wonderful example.

00:16:09.435 --> 00:16:11.954
And of course you're a lot younger
than I am, so you can still

00:16:11.954 --> 00:16:13.425
learn a whole bunch of languages.

00:16:13.875 --> 00:16:18.735
Uh, and I think it's just, if we can
infect more people with that enthusiasm

00:16:18.735 --> 00:16:24.285
and that attitude, more people will,
um, you know, benefit from the joys

00:16:24.615 --> 00:16:29.625
of learning 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, however
many languages they want, uh, for

00:16:29.625 --> 00:16:32.175
whatever reason that they're interested.

00:16:32.205 --> 00:16:35.445
And so I, you know, I thank you
for sharing that joy with, uh,

00:16:35.455 --> 00:16:36.795
the people that follow my channel.

00:16:36.915 --> 00:16:38.964
Maybe you want to give us a final comment.

00:16:39.814 --> 00:16:42.135
I mean, steve, I think
you're a perfect example.

00:16:42.135 --> 00:16:44.295
You have the joy as well, it's infectious.

00:16:44.325 --> 00:16:48.405
And it says a lot that you've
dedicated your life to inspiring

00:16:48.435 --> 00:16:51.765
other people to learn languages
and learning languages yourself.

00:16:51.795 --> 00:16:56.865
I mean, to me, it's a gateway
to living multiple lives.

00:16:56.895 --> 00:17:00.795
I always say that I'm like,
why live one life when you can

00:17:00.795 --> 00:17:02.805
live many, many, many more?

00:17:03.225 --> 00:17:07.194
There are people in my life who I
would have not met nor eould have

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:10.570
never, you know, become friends with,
if I didn't speak French or Ita...

00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:11.430
or whatever, you know?

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:17.339
Like there's such a missed
opportunity when we don't spend time

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:19.230
trying to understand one another.

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:20.970
It's like, yeah, sure
we have translations.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:24.960
We have all of these, you know,
services, but there's nothing

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.430
that beats that human connection.

00:17:26.849 --> 00:17:30.060
When you look at someone in their eyes
and speak their language and understand

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:35.220
them in their language, I can really
put in more time and energy into that.

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:39.030
I feel like my life will be a
million times more fulfilling.

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:40.980
Okay.

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:44.490
Well, on that note, thank you for
taking the time to visit with us.

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:47.520
